# DAY 2
R.append(("Anybody can learn the structures. Very few advisors will sit in a room and ask a question that makes a sixty-eight year old man think about something he has been avoiding.",
"""Anybody can learn the structures. Very few advisors will sit in a room and ask a question that makes a sixty-eight year old man think about something he has been avoiding.

Two questions I put to almost everybody, and I will use them in a first meeting before I have earned anything. What are your three greatest concerns about your life today. And what would bring you the greatest relief if it were solved today.

Watch what those do. Neither one mentions money, and both of them get answered in financial terms about half the time anyway, which tells you something. The other half of the time you learn that the thing keeping this man awake is a son, or a diagnosis he has not told anyone about, or a decision he made twenty years ago that he is still carrying. None of that is on the balance sheet, and all of it will determine whether the plan you build ever gets executed."""))

# DAY 3
R.append(("Your job is not to fill the well. It is to have something long enough to reach down into it.",
"""Your job is not to fill the well. It is to have something long enough to reach down into it.

The instrument I use is not complicated. If we were to meet three years from today, looking back over your life from now until then in any area, what must have happened by then for you to feel good about your progress. Then I write down whatever comes, and I do not improve it while it is being said.

Then I ask the spouse the same question. Separately, in full, with the same attention. That is the step almost everybody skips, and it is where most of the useful material comes from, because in a great many households one person has been answering for both of them for thirty years and nobody has ever tested whether the answer was accurate.

Notice what the three-year question does structurally. It does not ask what they want, which produces a list of positions. It asks what must have happened, which makes them describe a future rather than defend a preference."""))

# DAY 4
R.append(("So a long silence is evidence that your question worked.",
"""So a long silence is evidence that your question worked.

Here is a way to hear it that might change what you do with it. Tom's own image for this work is drawing something out of deep water. Deep water is not hidden water. What you are after is already in the room and already belongs to the family, and the only question is whether you brought anything long enough to reach it. A silence is the sound of the bucket going down.

Now here is what is happening in you."""))

# DAY 5
R.append(("Notice that all three come from the same place. All three are ways of protecting the meeting from uncertainty. Talking fills it, solving resolves it, and asking without listening performs the appearance of it without ever letting it in.",
"""Notice that all three come from the same place. All three are ways of protecting the meeting from uncertainty. Talking fills it, solving resolves it, and asking without listening performs the appearance of it without ever letting it in.

There is a second cost that is easy to miss. All three of them also stop you from doing the other half of the job, which is watching. You are supposed to be aware of how the conversation is unfolding. Are people engaged. Are they holding back. Is someone dominating. Part of your role is to gently guide that so everyone has a voice, and you cannot do any of it while you are the one talking. An advisor who is filling the room is by definition not reading it.

You are paying attention to both what is being said and how it is being said, and the second one is where the information usually is."""))

# DAY 6
R.append(("If you cannot tell which one you are looking at, you will apply the wrong intervention with real sincerity and it will not work, and you will conclude the family is not ready.",
"""If you cannot tell which one you are looking at, you will apply the wrong intervention with real sincerity and it will not work, and you will conclude the family is not ready.

The signs are not subtle once you know to look for them. Silence around important topics is one. Tension that has no clear explanation is another. And sometimes it shows up as confusion, where people are plainly holding different expectations and have never compared them.

None of those three will be reported to you. Nobody opens a review meeting by saying there is an unexplained tension in this family. You will have to notice it, and noticing is a different skill from asking."""))

# DAY 7
R.append(("That is what makes timing the most expensive of the four barriers. The other three at least feel like something. Discomfort feels like discomfort. Fear feels like fear. Deferral feels like agreement.",
"""That is what makes timing the most expensive of the four barriers. The other three at least feel like something. Discomfort feels like discomfort. Fear feels like fear. Deferral feels like agreement.

There is a version of this that runs underneath the stated reason, and it is worth naming because it changes what you say. Most people do not wait because the timing is wrong. They wait because they do not know how to start, and waiting is what people do when they lack an opening line. The stated reason is real and it is not the operative one.

Which means the fix is often not persuasion. It is supply. Hand them the first sentence and a date, and a surprising number of these deferrals end."""))

# DAY 8
R.append(("Every one of those silences is somebody protecting somebody. That is why they are so durable. You cannot shame a family out of a silence that is made of love.",
"""Every one of those silences is somebody protecting somebody. That is why they are so durable. You cannot shame a family out of a silence that is made of love.

What you can do is lower the cost of the first sentence. Not a difficult conversation. One small, thoughtful question that invites somebody to share without feeling pressured. What matters most to you right now. How are you thinking about the future. Then listen.

The goal in that first exchange is not to resolve anything. It is to begin, and to move a family from avoidance to engagement, which is a smaller move than it sounds and a harder one than it looks."""))

# DAY 9
R.append(("Deuteronomy 6 is where I would send a client who believes their children absorbed it. Impress them on your children. Talk about them when you sit at home and when you walk along the road. That is not a description of modeling. It is a description of speech, repeated, in ordinary circumstances, over time.",
"""There is a way to test an assumption without accusing anybody of anything, and it does not require a confrontation. You ask the parents for permission to talk to the children, and then you ask the children what they understand.

That sequence matters. Permission from the first generation first, then a promise of confidentiality to the second, then actual questions. Done in that order it is due diligence on the plan rather than a challenge to the parents, and most clients will agree to it once it is framed as making sure the plan lands the way they intend.

What comes back is almost never what the parents predicted. Sometimes the children know more than anyone thought. More often they know the values and nothing at all about the intentions, which is precisely the split this reading is about.

Deuteronomy 6 is where I would send a client who believes their children absorbed it. Impress them on your children. Talk about them when you sit at home and when you walk along the road. That is not a description of modeling. It is a description of speech, repeated, in ordinary circumstances, over time."""))

# DAY 10
R.append(("That is the first reason it compounds. Here is the second, and it is worse. As the stakes rise, the incentive to keep avoiding it rises too. A conversation that is easy to have is easy to have. A conversation that has become expensive is one everybody has a reason to defer again. The barrier feeds itself.",
"""That is the first reason it compounds. Here is the second, and it is worse. As the stakes rise, the incentive to keep avoiding it rises too. A conversation that is easy to have is easy to have. A conversation that has become expensive is one everybody has a reason to defer again. The barrier feeds itself.

And there is a third that has nothing to do with anybody's choices. The value of things moves. A business doubles, a piece of real estate triples, a trust stays flat, and an arrangement that was scrupulously fair on the day it was signed quietly stops being fair while everyone involved is doing exactly what they were supposed to do. Nobody has to behave badly for a plan to age into a grievance."""))

# DAY 11
R.append(("Nobody does the second one because you asked nicely. They do it because they have concluded it is safe.",
"""Nobody does the second one because you asked nicely. They do it because they have concluded it is safe.

Safety in a room is built out of three unglamorous behaviors, and none of them is a question. Listening without interrupting. Not reacting too quickly. And acknowledging what somebody says even when you do not agree with it.

That third one is the hardest and the most load bearing. Acknowledgment is not agreement, and most of us collapse the two, which is why we withhold the acknowledgment when we intend to push back. The family reads the withholding accurately. They conclude that saying something you dislike costs them your attention, and they stop saying things you might dislike.

When people feel heard, trust begins to build. That is the whole sequence, and it runs in that order rather than the other one."""))

# DAY 12
R.append(("Here is a test you can run on your own material. Read your standard opening out loud and ask what it invites. Most of ours invite a report. We ask how things have been, and we get a summary, because we asked for a summary.",
"""Here is a test you can run on your own material. Read your standard opening out loud and ask what it invites. Most of ours invite a report. We ask how things have been, and we get a summary, because we asked for a summary.

The opposite of pressure is not warmth. It is the removal of consequence. A family will hold back as long as they suspect that something said today becomes a commitment tomorrow, and the single most freeing sentence available to you is that no decisions are being made in this meeting.

That sentence sounds like a limitation on the meeting. It is the thing that makes the meeting possible, because it separates speaking from deciding, and most people can speak freely once those two have been pulled apart."""))

# DAY 13
R.append(("Simple is doing real work in that first sentence. Simple means one thing, wide, without a preferred answer.",
"""Simple is doing real work in that first sentence. Simple means one thing, wide, without a preferred answer.

There is a fourth thing that turns a good question into a bad one, and it is not in the question at all. It is what you do with the answer. If somebody gives you a wide answer and your follow-up returns to your own agenda, you have told them the width was decorative. The next answer will be narrower, and by the third one you are back to a report.

So the discipline is that the second question has to come out of their answer rather than out of your list. That is harder than it sounds, because your list is prepared and their answer is not.

Once people begin to feel comfortable, it becomes much easier to go deeper over time. A good start creates momentum, and momentum is the thing you are actually building in a first conversation."""))

# DAY 14
R.append(("Which means your job in a first family conversation is narrower than it looks. You are not trying to get everybody to open up. You are trying to get one person to, and to make sure that when they do, nothing bad happens to them.",
"""Which means your job in a first family conversation is narrower than it looks. You are not trying to get everybody to open up. You are trying to get one person to, and to make sure that when they do, nothing bad happens to them.

Consider how you would know it is working, because the marker is not what most advisors watch for. People are talking. They are giving you more than surface-level responses. They are listening to each other rather than to you. And there is a sense of forward movement even when it is slow.

None of those is a decision, and none of them would appear in a meeting summary. Progress here is measured by engagement rather than by speed, and a meeting that produced nothing but four people hearing each other for the first time is a meeting that worked."""))

# DAY 15
R.append(("So the question is not how to appear interested. It is whether you are.",
"""So the question is not how to appear interested. It is whether you are.

I want to be careful not to turn that into a counsel of despair, because it is not one. Posture is not fixed the way height is fixed. It moves, slowly, in response to what you actually do, which means the behaviors in the rest of this week are not a substitute for caring about these families. They are one of the ways people come to care.

Ask a family the three-year question forty times and something happens to you somewhere around the twelfth. You start wanting to know the answer. I would not have predicted that when I started, and it is the most reliable thing I have observed in advisors who make this shift."""))

# DAY 16
R.append(("The word I would draw your attention to hardest is identity. That is the one that turns a difficult conversation into an unrecoverable one. If a person's sense of who they are in this family is bound up in an asset or a role, then a proposal to change the arrangement is not a financial proposal. It is a proposal about them, and they will defend it accordingly.",
"""The word I would draw your attention to hardest is identity. That is the one that turns a difficult conversation into an unrecoverable one. If a person's sense of who they are in this family is bound up in an asset or a role, then a proposal to change the arrangement is not a financial proposal. It is a proposal about them, and they will defend it accordingly.

This is why the questions I ask about money are so often not about money. What do you hope this accomplishes in their lives. What amount would be a blessing to them and not a curse. Neither of those can be answered with a number, and both of them force the person to say out loud what the money is actually for, frequently for the first time.

Ron Blue puts the principle in a line I have quoted for years. You love your children equally, therefore you treat them uniquely. That sentence does more work in a distribution conversation than any model I have ever built."""))

